WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.633
PPO.

2
00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:04.819
One small trick keeps policy training stable.

3
00:00:04.819 --> 00:00:07.833
It is also the engine behind aligning chatbots.

4
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:14.967
Policy-gradient methods adjust the policy's weights directly, making good actions more probable.

5
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:18.733
Their curse: one oversized update can wreck the policy.

6
00:00:19.033 --> 00:00:23.567
And that broken policy collects the next batch of data, so it may never recover.

7
00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:28.000
PPO's one elegant move starts with a ratio.

8
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.433
New policy probability divided by old policy probability, for the same action.

9
00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:36.467
Then we clip that ratio to a narrow band.

10
00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:38.600
Epsilon is about zero point two.

11
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.300
Here is the clip as a curve.

12
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.967
The dashed line is the raw ratio, growing without limit.

13
00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:49.233
The clipped version goes flat beyond zero point eight and one point two.

14
00:00:49.533 --> 00:00:55.033
No batch, however extreme, can pull the policy outside the trust band around its old self.

15
00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:59.433
Two reasons this is the most important algorithm here.

16
00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:03.167
It is the default for continuous control and robotics.

17
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:05.744
And it is the engine of RLHF.

18
00:01:05.744 --> 00:01:12.967
The LLM is the policy, tokens are actions, and a reward model stands in for the environment.

19
00:01:14.167 --> 00:01:17.133
In Stable Baselines3 it's a single parameter.

20
00:01:17.433 --> 00:01:19.333
Clip range of zero point two.

21
00:01:19.633 --> 00:01:25.170
In the logs, watch clip fraction: the share of samples where the clip actually engaged.

22
00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:29.433
Healthy runs sit between about zero point one and zero point three.

23
00:01:30.633 --> 00:01:33.633
Remember the four models of RLHF?

24
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:36.590
Policy, reference, reward, and value.

25
00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:39.533
The optimizer holding it together is PPO.

26
00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:45.267
The clip keeps the LLM near its reference, so it doesn't collapse into reward-hacked gibberish.

27
00:01:45.567 --> 00:01:47.800
Build intuition on LunarLander first.

28
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:49.760
Let's recap.

29
00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.167
Big policy updates can wreck learning.

30
00:01:52.467 --> 00:01:55.700
PPO clips the probability ratio to a trust band.

31
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.200
It's the default for control, and the engine of RLHF.

32
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.213
Watch the clip fraction in your logs.

33
00:02:03.213 --> 00:02:04.167
Next up: A2C.
